26. DEFINITION OF DONE

The project is not complete merely because the interface looks good.

SalesInsight is considered functional when the following end-to-end workflow succeeds:

A new user opens the public website.
The user creates an account.
The application validates and securely stores the account.
The user verifies their email if verification is enabled.
The user logs in.
The application displays their dashboard.
The user uploads a CSV or XLSX sales dataset.
The system validates the file and records its metadata.
The user reviews the data-quality report.
The user performs supported cleaning operations.
The Python analytics engine processes the dataset.
The user sees calculated metrics and charts.
The user reviews the analysis history.
The user generates and downloads a report.
The user logs out and can log in again.
Another user cannot access the first user's private datasets or reports.
An administrator can manage permitted system functions.
Automated tests cover critical workflows and security boundaries.
The setup documentation allows another developer to run the project.
The repository contains no committed secrets.

Where email credentials, storage services, or external integrations are unavailable, provide a clearly documented local-development configuration rather than pretending the integration works.

27. STRICT ENGINEERING RULES

Follow these rules throughout implementation:

Inspect before modifying.
Build incrementally.
Prefer working, tested code over large volumes of generated code.
Do not leave core features as TODO comments or fake implementations.
Do not use hardcoded analytical results.
Do not use fake authentication.
Do not rely on frontend-only authorization.
Do not duplicate analytical calculations across services.
Do not expose database credentials or private API keys.
Do not silently discard data.
Do not fabricate business insights.
Do not skip database migrations.
Do not claim tests pass without running them.
Do not replace real error handling with generic success messages.
Do not create unnecessary services or dependencies.
Keep code modular, readable, and maintainable.
Document assumptions and technical decisions.
Prefer established, supported libraries.
Handle empty datasets and missing columns gracefully.
Report any blockers honestly.

After completing each phase:

Summarize files created or modified.
Explain the implementation.
List commands to run.
Run the relevant tests.
Report test results honestly.
Explain known limitations.
Identify the next phase.

Do not attempt to implement every phase in one uncontrolled code-generation operation.

